ATTACK 1 -- flake probability of TestGuardrailOptimizerRowWording_PlanDesignVsFundingRisk:
  Fixture: PortfolioValue 150000, MonthlyLivingExpenses 10000 (Go-Go 1.00, No-Go
  0.50 from age 66/year 1), floor_monthly_real=7500, target_success_pct=50.
  Ran the test 20x total (-count=1, separate invocations): 20/20 PASS, 0 failures.
  Seam check: handleGuardrailOptimizer (handlers_guardrail_optimizer.go:90-131)
  parses ONLY floor_monthly_real/target_success_pct/request_id from the form and
  builds models.GuardrailOptimizerRequest{FloorMonthlyReal, TargetSuccessPct} --
  Seed is left zero even though the model has a Seed field, so
  analysis.EffectiveSeed always auto-seeds from time.Now().UnixNano() at this
  call site. This is PRE-EXISTING, unchanged production behaviour (confirmed
  identical in attempt 1) -- every other MC-driven test in this file
  (TestGuardrailOptimizerPlanFloorNoticeBelow/Above, both already accepted at
  attempt 1) already relies on the same auto-seed path, so this is not a new
  risk class introduced by attempt 2, and there is no deterministic seed knob
  the handler-level test could have used instead.
  Economic sanity check on why the margin is wide rather than borderline:
  HorizonMinYears/MaxYears = max(10, ProjectionYears +/- LongevityVariation) =
  max(10, 3+/-5) = 10 in both directions (DefaultMonteCarloConfig().LongevityVariation
  = 5), so every validation run spans >=10 years. Withdrawing $90k-120k/yr
  (Go-Go $10000/mo, later No-Go $5000/mo, plus the $7500 floor clamp) against a
  $150000 starting portfolio for >=10 years is economically indistinguishable
  from certain depletion under any realistic Monte Carlo return distribution --
  reaching even a 50% success rate is not a near-miss, it is off by orders of
  magnitude. This matches the test author's own framing (comment: "verified
  empirically stable (0.00% for every row across repeated runs)"; "NOT
  mathematically airtight" but "the same standard the optimizer's existing
  MC-driven tests already rely on"). Verdict: real but negligible flake risk,
  consistent with pre-existing test design in this same file; not a new defect
  class introduced by attempt 2.

